{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions","id":1334604,"title":"Lex Magica Questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-09-09T04:10:44-07:00","id":1334604,"title":"Lex Magica Questions","content":"I am basing this off p. 69 in the book so if there is more detailed information elsewhere that is up to date with the 2nd edition, let me know...<br /> <br /> So as I understand it the Gold Laws are kind of the &quot;Constitution of the Awakened World&quot; and you can go pretty much anywhere in the world and expect these laws to be respected. &quot;They're rare&quot; is kind of a weird way of putting it, I imagine this means that they are rarely invoked and there just aren't very many of them. They cover<ul><li>Mages have the right to study magic, so long as they do not interfere with other mages' studies (my right to swing my arm ends at your nose)</li> <li>The right for a mage to maintain a Sanctum and control Hallows without fear of trespass</li> <li>The right for a mage to be recognized for their mastery of the Arcana</li> </ul>Given how universal the Gold Laws are, I would think it would be easier to just innumerate them in the book?<br /> <br /> Also, the Silver Laws are the level where local Consilium Charters are enumerated, suggesting that a person living outside of a Consilium in theory should be protected by the Gold Laws. Yet on the whole they seem pretty weak. Suppose there is a hermit who holds a sanctum out in the wilderness far from any Consilium's jurisdiction, and a mage barges into his sanctum, steals his Mana, destroys his research and writes &quot;Yo mastery of Death magic ain't shit!&quot; on the walls. Who exactly is this hermit going to complain to? Who is going to enforce the Gold Laws?<br /> <br /> Incidentally, <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">beyond insults, what exactly does being &quot;recognized for their mastery of the Arcana&quot; really entail? </span></span></span><br /> <br /> I suppose he could bring his complaint to the next Convocation (in the style of ancient Vikings bringing their legal complaints to the Thing) but beyond that it's kind of the Wild West, I suppose... or is that maybe more at the Silver Law level?<br /> <br /> So what level are the Precepts at? The text is pretty vague on this, almost suggesting that they are a mixture of Silver and Bronze, but given how universal they are, it seems like they should be Silver at the minimum. There are probably numerous Bronze laws regarding how they should be implemented, maybe that's what they are referring?<br /> <br /> Also, has anyone come up with some interesting Silver laws for your games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1078,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","user":"Froggy711","date":". 09-09-2019, 07:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-09T05:27:18-07:00","id":1334615,"title":"","content":"If you\u2019re living outside of a Consilium you can call on your Order, a Lictor, or go suck eggs depending on your political capital.<br /> <br /> Some of your political capital is almost an automatic gain via gaining skill in an arcana. If there\u2019s a Death thing going on and you\u2019re the best Death Mage available your word in it gets more weight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-09T12:15:29-07:00","id":1334694,"title":"","content":"If the hermit is my Mentor, and you come into my Consilium, and if I have any credibility at all, I will tell everyone what you did, and at the very least that would be an issue you would have to deal with. I don't imagine any Consilium taking someone breaking a Gold Law lightly, even if outside of their jurisdiction. I do see some rationalizing it away and letting you off with a slap on the wrist if you have enough political capital and a veneer of extenuating circumstances, but they would at least go through the motions of Doing Something.<br /> <br /> The reason I think they are not enumerated in the book is to give you leeway in how you want to actually implement them in your own game, and if you want to make some up on your own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12673-ew-matias","name":"EW-Matias","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"245"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-10T11:45:29-07:00","id":1334909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am basing this off p. 69 in the book so if there is more detailed information elsewhere that is up to date with the 2nd edition, let me know...<br /> <br /> So as I understand it the Gold Laws are kind of the &quot;Constitution of the Awakened World&quot; and you can go pretty much anywhere in the world and expect these laws to be respected. &quot;They're rare&quot; is kind of a weird way of putting it, I imagine this means that they are rarely invoked and there just aren't very many of them. They cover<ul><li>Mages have the right to study magic, so long as they do not interfere with other mages' studies (my right to swing my arm ends at your nose)</li> <li>The right for a mage to maintain a Sanctum and control Hallows without fear of trespass</li> <li>The right for a mage to be recognized for their mastery of the Arcana</li> </ul><br /> Given how universal the Gold Laws are, I would think it would be easier to just innumerate them in the book?</div> </div> </div> </div>All of them come from the Time Before, which means they are written in High Speech (which may be difficult to translate), probably involve obscure concepts that were lost to history, invoke other laws that are no longer known, etc. I doubt they are consistent laws \u2013 most likely they are loose approximations how did the perfect mage society look like before it was erased from time. Add an occasional fabrication and you can bet every Silver Ladder and Mysterium Caucus has its own, slightly different interpretation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, the Silver Laws are the level where local Consilium Charters are enumerated, suggesting that a person living outside of a Consilium in theory should be protected by the Gold Laws. Yet on the whole they seem pretty weak. Suppose there is a hermit who holds a sanctum out in the wilderness far from any Consilium's jurisdiction, and a mage barges into his sanctum, steals his Mana, destroys his research and writes &quot;Yo mastery of Death magic ain't shit!&quot; on the walls. Who exactly is this hermit going to complain to? Who is going to enforce the Gold Laws?</div> </div> </div> </div>If they are nobodies, they are in a difficult situation, especially if the perpetrator is a member. The Hierarch may not be very keen to send a Sentinel in the middle of nowhere, so the actual investigation will probably be delayed or delegated to some nobodies (here's an opportunity for your cabal). And even if the offending mage is guilty as sin, the actual punishment may involve nothing more than a very stern talk. Yes, the Gold Laws are important, but many mages won't risk upsetting a good-standing member for some nobody living in the wild.<br /> <br /> Of course, if the victim has some actual pull in his order or the Consilium itself, this is a very different situation. That's why mages are heavily incentivized to participate in Awakened politics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Incidentally, <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">beyond insults, what exactly does being &quot;recognized for their mastery of the Arcana&quot; really entail? </span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose the particular interpretation may depend of the Consillium and the Silver Ladder Caucus. Most likely this matters when assigning political positions \u2013 a Master omitted for the position of a Councilor for someone less skilled could probably object. Some Consilia may expect certain etiquette when addressing more experienced members. There is also the Right of Emeritus, one of the five Great Laws your cabal can voluntarily obey, which demands that those who earned respect need to be treated with such (even if you're at war with them, or they are a Rapt Reaper Lich who immolates one Sleeper soul daily for their experiments)<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13425-gantolandon","name":"Gantolandon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-10T12:04:30-07:00","id":1334919,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13425-gantolandon\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"13425\" data-vbnamecard=\"13425\">Gantolandon</a> Mages suffer no difficulty in understanding High Speech.<br /> <br /> The rest is fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-17T07:09:32-07:00","id":1336205,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So as I understand it the Gold Laws are kind of the &amp;quot;Constitution of the Awakened World&amp;quot; and you can go pretty much anywhere in the world and expect these laws to be respected. &amp;quot;They're rare&amp;quot; is kind of a weird way of putting it, I imagine this means that they are rarely invoked and there just aren't very many of them. They cover<ul><li>Mages have the right to study magic, so long as they do not interfere with other mages' studies (my right to swing my arm ends at your nose)</li> <li>The right for a mage to maintain a Sanctum and control Hallows without fear of trespass</li> <li>The right for a mage to be recognized for their mastery of the Arcana</li> </ul><br /> Given how universal the Gold Laws are, I would think it would be easier to just innumerate them in the book?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Gold Laws are general legal principles, even above constitutional laws. They are like, the right &quot;to claim and make use of private property&quot;, &quot;to seek mediation and arbitration from a judicial institution&quot;, &quot;to freedom of movement and speech&quot; and so on, except regarding magic and the mysteries. <br /> <br /> <br /> According the the Silver Ladder book, a Golden Law status is only granted by Artifact (comes from the Supernal or from the Time Before), by Harmony (is geographically ubiquitous) or by Supremacy (it hasn't been challenged and it's universally accepted by Convocations). The Gold Laws include the Great Rights of mages, and the right of mages to challenge one another to legally binding duels on neutral ground (though not the specifics). Most Consilia acknowledge these Gold Laws in their own Charters, making them also Silver in the specifics of each law.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Also, the Silver Laws are the level where local Consilium Charters are enumerated, suggesting that a person living outside of a Consilium in theory should be protected by the Gold Laws. Yet on the whole they seem pretty weak. Suppose there is a hermit who holds a sanctum out in the wilderness far from any Consilium's jurisdiction, and a mage barges into his sanctum, steals his Mana, destroys his research and writes &amp;quot;Yo mastery of Death magic ain't shit!&amp;quot; on the walls. Who exactly is this hermit going to complain to? Who is going to enforce the Gold Laws?</div> </div> </div> </div>A Consilium charter (especially one drafted in a Convocation) is more like a formal Constitution, and it's considered Silver Laws. Many old Bronze Laws become Silver when a Consilium legislates them as a bill of rights, or when a Consilium is formed and its constituents try to work on their predecessors' body of law. However, the Lex Magica operates on the general principles of recognition and precedent, and the ability to uphold the law depends more on who can enforce it and to whom, than its abstract legal value. In the example you mention, the particulars are highly dependent of context and the actors involved. Is the hermit an outlaw, or is it a member of the Consilium? If the latter, is he a member in good standing, or is he a known apostate? Does the perpetrator belongs to the Consilium (does a subject under the law), or is he a Seer? As others have said in this thread, the hermit could ask for a Lictor, go to the Consilium or to his own Caucus, or wait for the next Convocation to make his case there, but the answer and the type of justice he may get can be completely different depending on where he goes for satisfaction.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Incidentally, <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">beyond insults, what exactly does being &amp;quot;recognized for their mastery of the Arcana&amp;quot; really entail? </span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Let's suppose you are mage, a master in the Arcana, in a new Consilium, fresh off the boat. You keep a low profile, give your Shadow name to the Herald when word reach him about you and for the most part you keep to yourself. No one's gonna put a gun on your head and make you confess about your magical capabilities, but no one's gonna trust you either: you are an unknown quantity, an inconsecuentual solitary at best, or a dangerous loner at worst. You get from the Consilium what you put into it, so to speak. Now, let's suppose that you get tired of being left out of polite society, you go ask for a Council hearing and demonstrate a Death making spell in front of the Ruling Council. Now you are a known quantity, someone worth recognition as a master. The Right of Emeritus will be upheld by many cabals that will treat you with deference and respect. You will be expected at any important Consilium gathering, and your expert opinion will be required on any mysteries concerning death and the dead. A few apprentices will petition to be tutored by you on the Death Arcanum, and and other masters will start offering small favors and see if you correspond them the same way. The more you put into society, the more you reap later.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1334604-lex-magica-questions#post1334604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> So what level are the Precepts at? The text is pretty vague on this, almost suggesting that they are a mixture of Silver and Bronze, but given how universal they are, it seems like they should be Silver at the minimum. There are probably numerous Bronze laws regarding how they should be implemented, maybe that's what they are referring?</div> </div> </div> </div>According to the Silver Ladder book, the precepts are just common sense Bronze Laws, so the thearchs try to squeeze them into Consilium Charters (Silver) whenever they can get away with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}